{"189862": "A g\nX'. =~\\,~r=\n\nSTATE OF MAINE\nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, CONSERVATION & FORESTRY\nOFFICE or THE COMMISSIONER 22 STATE HOUSE STATION AUGUSTA, MAINE 04333\n\nJANET T, MILLS GOVERNOR\n\nAMANDA E. BEAL\nCOMMISSIONER\n\nTESTIMONY BEFORE THE JOINT STANDING COMMITTEE ON AGRICULTURE, CONSERVATION AND FORESTRY\nNEITHER FOR NOR AGAINST LD 1323\n\nAn Act to Prohibit the Use ofNeonicotinoid Pesticides and the Use and Sale of\nNeonicotinoid-Treated Seeds\n\nApril 15, 2025\n\nSenator Talbot Ross, Representative Pluecker, and members of the Joint Standing Committee on\nAgriculture, Conservation and Forestry, I am Alex Peacock, Director of the Board of Pesticides Control (BPC) in the Department of Agriculture, Conservation and Forestry (DACF). I am here speaking neither for nor against LD 1323, An Act to Prohibit the Use ofNeonicotinoid Pesticides\nand the Use and Sale ofNeonicotinoid-Treated Seeds.\nBPC is Maine's lead agency for pesticide oversight. Policy decisions are made by a sevenmember public board. DACF's primary concern with LD 1323 is process oriented, as we believe that the BPC is the appropriate body to address the concerns and goals raised by LD 1323. BPC's\nstructure, members' experience, and its required robust public input process make it well-suited\nto address the issues presented in this bill. It could thoroughly assess the concepts presented, such as considering:\n0 Available data, or resolving data gaps, that can best inform the potential application and impact of prohibitions or allowances of use presented in this bill;\nI How to identify low or lower-risk alternative approaches to the management of pests\nroutinely controlled with neonicotinoids;\n0 How to prevent transition to the use of pesticides with greater toxicity; and 0 How to identify and best address other unintended consequences.\nIt should also be noted that BPC has unique primacy over pesticide regulatory actions pursuant\nto the Federal Insecticide Fungicide and Rodenticide Act (FIFRA) Sections 26 & 27 (40 CFR\nPart 173). However, LD 1323 removes the BPC as the primary authority goveming the use and\ndistribution of neonicotinoid pesticides. Instead, it places responsibility on the Commissioners of\nDACF and the Department of Environmental Protection to determine emergency agricultural\nexemptions. This could jeopardize Maine's primacy over pesticide regulations and maintenance\nof BPC's cooperative agreement with EPA by usurping the Board's rulemaking powers under 7\n\nHARLOW Burrnmo 18 ELKINS LANE\nAUGUSTA, MAINE\n\nVlfrlalne\n\n7�;;\"{::,'i;:,'re\ngonservation\n& Forestry\n\nPHONE! (207) 287-3200 FAX; (207) 287-2400\nWEB: WWW.MAINE.GOV/DACF\n\nMRS �610 and returning regulatory oversight to the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency\n(EPA).\nFurthermore, LD 1323 includes restrictions on applying pesticides during \"bloom\" for\n\"neonicotinoid-treated seed for soybeans or for any crop in the cereal grains crop group.\" This\nmay be redundant language since most pesticide labels, which are legally binding and must be\nfollowed by anyone using them, already have language that restricts or prohibits applications to blooming crops or other flowering plants. These restrictions on pesticide labels are in place to protect pollinators and non-target organisms from exposure to any pesticide, including\nneonicotinoids.\nThe bill also raises concerns regarding exemptions. First, a lack of clear definitions for \"integrated pest management\" and \"pest risk assessment report\" creates uncertainty in the exemption process. Second, the requirement to submit detailed geographic and product usage\ninformation to the ACF committee may deter agricultural producers from seeking exemptions\ndue to privacy concerns.\nSimilarly, DACF is concerned that the timeframe for compliance -- January 2026 -- is unrealistic.\nFinding alternative seeds for the next growing season could be challenging for producers, as\nwould be identifying and procuring the necessary alternative crop treatments that some would need to revert to if no longer able to utilize treated seeds.\nFrom a recent conversation with the bill sponsor, we do understand that she is willing to address\nsome of the concems in our testimony and to consider clarifying any crops that would be exempt\nfrom the scope of this proposed legislation. Should this bill move forward, we look forward to\ncollaborating with the sponsor and Committee throughout the legislative process to ensure key\nconcerns raised by DACF and others you will hear from today are resolved.\nThank you for your time. I am happy to answer any questions now or at the work session.", "189847": "Testimony in Support of Neonicotinoid Insecticides\nBy Alvin Winslow, CCA, NMP, BS, MS Owner/Manager of Winslow Agriculture LLC\n4/15/25\nChairperson and Members of the Committee,\nGood afternoon. My name is Alvin Winslow, and I am a certified crop advisor and the owner of Winslow Agriculture LLC located in New Gloucester. I provide crop consulting services and\ncontract research in Maine, working with 17,000 acres of potatoes, 10,000 acres of corn, 10,000 acres of grass, and 600 acres of small vegetable production. Like a honey bee, I work for the farmers, and they take good care of me!\nThank you for the opportunity to testify today in support of the continued use and careful regulation of neonicotinoid insecticides. I believe that these insecticides represent one of the safest and most effective classes of chemistry available to farmers today. The last couple of\nyears we have been working towards lowering our Environmental Impact of our spray programs\nby selecting pesticides with a low EIQ numbers. These numbers are available in the Cornell\nIntegrated Crop and Pest Management Guidelines. When you consider the low EIQ numbers for neonicotinoids vs some of the older chemistry, it's easy to see how we can lower our environmental impact with these products. When used according to guidelines, neonicotinoids\nA\ncan effectively protect crops from pests, and improve the planet. Those of us who have been around long enough, I started working as commercial pesticide applicator in 1992, before we had neonics, and I can tell you firsthand that prior to neonics we were killing almost every\nbeneficial insect and pollinator with the old chemicals. Neonics were a big step forward, and continue to be an important part of integrated pest management.\nI would like to emphasize that with responsible application and adherence to safety protocols, neonicotinoids do not pose significant risks to our beekeepers or the environment. Concerns regarding neonicotinoids are not new; approximately 15 years ago, this issue was heavily debated across the country. I recall attending the Pollinator Health and Safety Conference in Portland, Maine, in November of 2014, where they addressed concerns about the effects of neonicotinoids on honeybees. Scientific research presented at that conference demonstrated that the primary causes of colony collapse include existing parasites, particularly Varroa mites; viruses and diseases affecting the bees; poor nutrition due to limited pollen sources; lack of genetic diversity; and stress from transportation and overcrowding. The evidence clearly indicated that neonicotinoids were not responsible for colony collapse. The big surprise at that conference was that the miticides being used to control the Varro mites, when mixing two products together trying to get rid of the mites, was actually creating a toxic cocktail that was\ncontributing to colony collapse --- a good reminder of how important it is to follow the label!\nNeonicotinoids are a vital tool in modern agriculture, ofiering effective protection against a wide range of harmful pests while minimizing the need for frequent applications. Because they are often applied as seed treatments, they target pests during the early stages of crop development, reducing the overall environmental footprint compared to traditional foliar sprays.\nI would like to offer as testimony my observations about the importance of neonicotinoid seed\ntreatments. Seed treatment technology is amazing in that it places a very small amount of chemical in exactly the right place targeting specific pests and utilizing a fraction of the material\n\nthat would be needed for alternative treatments. To illustrate I think I'm the last person in the\nstate of Maine who was calibrating Lorsban applicators on corn planters. Imagine putting down l0 or 12 lbs. of Lorsban per acre, yes it stinks and it was miserable to work with but if we didn't\ndo something the corn seed would be destroyed by seed corn maggots, wire worms, a whole variety of white grubs and other small soil insects that were hungry and eager to eat your corn seed. With the invention of neonicotinoid seed treatments a very small amount of chemical\ndirectly on the seed prevented all these pests, our cornfields tell the story - we have beautiful, even uniform stands of corn when we protect the seed. Seven years ago, I received 3 phone calls\none in Vermont one in New Hampshire and one in Maine all three farms had significant stand losses in their comfields. When I investigated these problems I found all of the old pests\nmentioned above dining on their seeds. It turned out that the common factor was a\nmisapplication of the seed treatment on a particular batch of seed each of the farmers recalled that when they opened the bag the seed treatment had fallen off the seed and was laying in the\nbottom of the bag like a powder. When they planted this untreated seed the pests attacked. This\nis what will happen if you ban neonicotinoid treated seeds and not just corn but a whole host of\nsmall vegetable production will be lost or we may have to revert to using the old chemicals that\nare worse for the farmers and worse for the environment and definitely worse for the bees.\nResearch and real-world usage have shown that, when used according to label instructions and integrated into a broader pest management strategy, neonicotinoids pose a low risk to pollinators and non-target species. Ongoing advances in application techniques, seed coatings, and risk\nmitigation efforts continue to improve their safety profile.\nRather than banning or overly restricting this important tool, the focus should be on sciencebased regulation, farmer education, and continued investment in monitoring and research. With\nresponsible stewardship, we can balance the needs of agriculture with the health of ecosystems\nand pollinator populations.\nI urge this committee to support policies that preserve access to neonicotinoids while promoting responsible use. Farmers need tools that are both effective and sustainable, and neonics have proven themselves to be exactly that.\nThank you for your time and attention. I am happy to answer any questions.\nSincerely, Alvin Winslow\nOwner/Manager, Winslow Agriculture LLC", "10044409": "Testimony of Andrew Marshall on LD1323, 4/15/25\nMy name is Andrew Marshall and I am from Montville, in Waldo County, where my family and I operate Dorolenna Farm, a vegetable and poultry operation. I am also a visiting professor of biology and environmental studies at Colby College in Waterville. I am here to testify in favor of LD 1323, because I have seen the data and read the studies, and I am deeply concerned about the impacts that these materials have had on ecosystems (pollinators in particular, but also other classes of beneficial organisms such as natural enemies) in a very short period of time - impacts that are well-known and have been described by others today. It is my considered opinion that we cannot afford to continue to design agricultural systems to be reliant on such toxic materials. I have particular concerns about the systemic use of these materials.\nAs a farmer, I understand and respect the position of those who are in opposition to a wholesale ban on neonics for certain crops. Perhaps such an approach is too blunt an instrument to achieve the goal of moving past our reliance on toxics in agriculture. But as an ecologist, I truly believe that the time has come to take bold and decisive action to incentivise those who are making decisions in our agricultural system - including farmers, researchers, service providers, and regulators - to continue moving towards a more ecologically rational approach to designing and managing our future farming systems. Thank you for your time.", "189854": "Testimony of Andrew B. Plant, Plant Pathologist, Maine Potato Board to the Committee on\nAgriculture, Conservation and Forestry April 15, 2025\nLD 1323 - An Act to Prohibit the Use of Neonicotinoid Pesticides and the Use and\nSale of Neonicotinoid-treated Seeds\nSenator Talbot Ross, Representative Pluecker and members of the Agriculture, Conseryation and\nForestry Committee, I am Andrew Plant, Plant Pathologist for the Maine Potato Board. I am here today to speak in opposition to LD 1323.\nPotato Virus Y (PVY) is a mechanically and aphid transmitted disease of potatoes. It is one of\nthe top reasons for seed lot rejection and down-grading in the world, and the top reason locally in\nthe state of Maine. PVY can cause substantial direct and indirect economic loss to potato\ngrowers and processors due to rejection and downgrading of seed lots, yield drag associated with planting infected seed and current-season spread, and quality issues in tablestock and processing\npotatoes making tubers and finished product umnarketable. PVY has become increasingly\nproblematic in the past fifteen years owing in large part to its shifting population dynamics to\nless symptomatic recombinant strains, and rejection levels have doubled in average since new\nstrains were first detected in our production area around 2010.\nBecoming less symptomatic has removed or severely blunted two important tools from our industry's tool chest in which to manage the problem. Field certification, by which State inspectors visually assess fields for level of virus infection and remove them from seed certification if excessive, and roguing, by which trained farm workers scout and remove symptomatic plants from seed fields so as to prevent further spread of the virus.\nCurrent PVY management focuses on a multi-tiered approach consisting of robust post-harvest\nlaboratory testing in order to plant low inoculum seed, using systemic insecticides at planting to control potato-colonizing aphids, and frequent (every 7 days or less), spraying of stylet oils beginning at 20-30% crop emergence and continuing until vine kill to control spread from non-\ncolonizing aphids.\nNon-colonizing aphids are those that don't settle to feed on the potato crop, but will repeatedly\nprobe the crop to assess its suitability to feed, and that is all the time PVY needs to spread from\nplant- to- plant. These non-colonizing aphids come from any surrounding vegetation, which in large part for the potato industry are rotation crops to include soybeans, com, and small grains. Each of these crops has its own suite of aphid colonizers (such as corn leaf aphid, soybean aphid, English grain aphid, bird cherry oat aphid etc) that will have direct and indirect impacts on both their main host as well as their neighboring potato fields.\nRestricting the use of neonic insecticides, whether directly to potato crops, or to the rotation crops that are utilized throughout potato production will directly affect the success of our State's potato production. It will lead to decreased yields, decreased quality, decreased marketability,\n\nincreased importation of seed and the concomitant risk of quarantine pest introduction, and an increased cost of production.\nRemoving the neonicotinoid tool from our tool chest will likely lead to more frequent applications of less effective insecticides resulting in an overall increase in amount of active\ningredients applied to our crops.\nI've limited this testimony to speak only about a singular aspect of this proposed prohibition. In brief, consideration to finding control alternatives and to ascertain the amount (both in scope and quantity) of active ingredients required to suitably replace neonicotinoid insecticides will be\nnecessarily as extensive as it's labeled uses: Aphids, Colorado potato beetles, flea beetles,\nleafhoppers, cereal leaf beetles, psyllids, wireworrns, and seed maggots.\nI ask that you vote ought not to pass on LD 1323. Thank you for your time today and I will be\nhappy to answer any questions you have.", "189857": "Committee on Agriculture, Conservation 8c Forestry\n% Legislative Information Office\n100 State House Station\nAugusta, ME 04333\n\nMAINEQ\nAUDUBON\n\nApril 15, 2025\nRE: LD 1323, An Act to Prohibit the Use of Neonicotinoid Pesticides and the Use and Sale of\nNeonicotinoid-treated Seeds\nDear Sen. Talbot Ross, Rep. Pluecker, and Members of the ACF Committee:\nMy name is Ania Wright, and I am Maine Audubon's Policy Advocate. Maine Audubon is a Wildlife\nconservation non-profit -- we fulfill our mission to \"conserve Maine wildlife and wildlife habitat\" by engaging people of all ages in nature through a science-based approach to education, conservation, and\nadvocacy. On behalf of Maine Audubon and our 30,000 members, supporters, and volunteers, thank you for the opportunity to submit testimony in support of LD 1323, An Act to Prohfltit the Use of\nNe0nz'c0tz'n0z'd Pesticides and the Use and Sale ofNe0nz'c0tz'n oid-trea ted Seeds.\n\nWe ur3e You to suPP ort LD 1323, a critical steP in safe8uarding Maine's ecosYstems, wildlife, and\npublic health from the pervasive and well-documented harms of neonicotinoid insecticides, also called \"neonics.\" Though effective at managing pests, neonics pose threats to Maine wildlife, pollinators, and farmers due to their indiscriminate nature and pervasiveness in the environment. In 2021, The 130th Legislature banned the use of some of the most prevalent neonics (dinotefuran, clothianidin,\nimidacloprid, and thiamethoxam) in most residential uses with the passage of LD 155.1 LD 1323\nupdates the current law to prohibit the application ofall neonics to ornamental plants, plants during bloom, and certain cereal crops, as well as prohibiting the use of neonic-coated soybean and cereal crop\nseeds.\n\nIn addition to Maine Audubon's written testimony, please see the separately submitted testimony\nfrom 22 environmental, public health, and social justice organizations in support of LD 1323,\nand a petition signed by nearly 850 Maine Audubon members and supporters.\n\n1 LD 155, Resolve, Directing the Board of Pesticides Control To Prohibit the Use of Certain Neonicotinoids for\nOutdoor Residential Use (RESLV Chapter 33)\nwww.mainelegislature.org/legis/bills/getPDF.asQ1pape1=HP0l1 1&ite1n=5&snum=l 30\n\n20 Gilsland Farm Road, Falmouth, Maine 04105\n\n'\n\n(207) 781-2330\n\n'\n\nmaineauclubonorg\n\nNeonics are a class of synthetic pesticides that are widely used to eliminate pests in agriculture, horticulture, and in some pet flea and tick treatments. They are the most prevalently used class of insecticides in the United States, and are popular due to their relatively low cost and high effectiveness of dealing with historically difficult pests. Unlike other pesticides, neonics are systemic, meaning that they are taken up by plants from the soil and become present in plant material and pollen.\nDespite their effectiveness, neonic threats outweigh their benefits. Over 90% of neonics applied as\ntreatments leach into soil, air, and water, contaminating non-target plants, wildflowers, and ecosystems.' The planting process itself emits neonic dust clouds that drift into neighboring lands and\nWaterways, putting native flora and fauna at risk.\nNeonic effectiveness is indiscriminate and kills not only pest insects, but beneficial pollinators, other insects, and wildlife that eat affected plants and insects. Perhaps most widely known, neonic use is\nattributed to mass pollinator declines which threaten our food system and farmers. On the\nsubsequent pages, please see in depth descriptions of effects that neonics have been found to have on various pollinators, amphibians, birds, endangered species, and even mammals.\nFurthermore, these pesticides are not necessary nor economically viable in all of the wide array of applications that neonics are currently being used. Peer-reviewed research demonstrates that\nneonicotinoid seed treatments for corn, soybean, and other cereal crops offer little to no economic advantage for farmers. According to a 2020 Cornell University review of over 250 field trials, 89% of corn studies and 82-89% of soybean studies showed no yield increase from using neonics. Even compared to no insecticide use at all, the vast majority of trials found no benefit. Farmers frequently do not know their seeds are treated, and untreated seeds are often not commercially available or have to be specifically sought out. Another study from Penn State University showed that\nusing neonic-coated soybean seeds actually decreased yield because they reduced beneficial predators?'\nThe Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) itself concluded that neonic-treated soybean seeds offer \"no overall net income benefit.\"`* In most cases, these pesticides are used as preventative\nmeasures, not as responses to specific pest problems.\n\n2C. Krupke & J. Tooker. (Dec 2020). Beyond the Headlines.\" The Influence ofInsurance Pest Management on an\nUnseen, Silent Entomological Majority. Frontiers in Sustainable Food Systems. doi.org/10.3389/fsufs.2020.595855\n3 M. Douglas & J. Tooker. (Dec 2014) Neonicotinoid insecticide travels through a soilfood chain, disrupting\nbiological control ofnon-target pests and decreasing soya bean yield. Journal of Applied Ecology. doiorg/10.1111/1365-266412372\n4 EPA (March 2025). Benefits ofNeonicotinoid Seed Treatments to Soybean Production. EPA.\nwww.epa.gov/polIinator-protection/benefits-neonisotinoid-seed-treatments-soybean-production\n\ngo Gileland F211111 Road, Faliiiouth, l\\/i;s1inr~;- Qt-3105\n\n(;;z<37) 781\"�:g,;3�1\n\n111ai21eat1di1b011.c�rg\n\nOther jurisdictions have restricted or prohibited the use of neonics and treated seeds, but their farms\ncontinue to thrive. Similar laws to LD 1323 have been passed in New York (in 2023)5 and Vermont (in\n2024).` Globally, the European Commission,7 Quebec,8 and Ontario9 have created restrictions on neonic coated seeds and use.\nWILDLIFE 8: ECOSYSTEM IMPACTS\nAquatic Ecosystems Neonicotinoids are water-soluble and easily enter aquatic ecosystems through runoff, posing threats to aquatic ecosystems, invertebrates, and amphibians. Once introduced into water systems, neonics persist and can bioaccumulate. Exposure to neonics can be lethal to invertebrate species, and can lead to sublethal effects including reduced feeding and reproduction, reduced growth, and immobility.\" Over time, these impacts can harm entire populations and alter food webs,\" as well as multiplying existing threats due to an already weakened population.\nAmphibians, which are particularly vulnerable to environmental toxins due to their permeable skin and cutaneous respiration, are also vulnerable to neonic toxicity. Studies have shown that neonic exposure in amphibians can lead to abnormal behaviors, disruption of developmental processes,\nincreased predation, neurological issues, and heightened mortality.\" One recent study showed a 10% decrease in larval survival to metamorphosis when tadpoles were exposed to imidacloprid. 15.7% of\nindividuals developed undifferentiated or hermaphroditic gonads--indicating endocrine disruption.\"\n\n5 Office of Govemor Kathy Hochul. (2024, April ll). Governor Hochul signs Birds and Bees Act, nation-leading\n\nlegislation to protect New Yorkers and pollinators.\n\n�\n\nwww.governor.ny�gov/news/governor-hochul-signs-birds-and-bees-act-nation-leading-legislation-protect-new-yorke\n\nrs-and\n6 National Caucus of Environmental Legislators. (2024, June 20). Vermont becomes second state to enact sweeping\n\npollinator protections.\n\nwww.ncelenviro.org/articles/vermont-becomessecond-state-to-enact-sweeping;pollinator-protections/\n7 European Commission. (Jul 2013). Bee Health: EU takes additional measures on pesticides to better protect\nEurope's bees. ec,_t;ur0pa.eu/conmzission/IQ;esscoruer/tleLg_il/e/1[ip_13_7Q�\n8 R. Malfi. (May 2024). What We Can Learn From Qu�bec `s Success With Regulating Pesticide-Treated Seed.\nXerces Society. /xercesorgfblog/what-we-camlearn-from-quebees-success-with-regulating;pesticide-treated-seed 9 Government of Ontario (n.d.) Neonicotinoid rules for growers. w_my,ontgrio.ca/pagefneonicotinoid-iiules-growers `� Xerces Society for Invertebrate Conservation. (2021). Insecticide Seed Treatments Threaten\n\nMidwestern Waterways. www.xerces.org/sites/default/files/21-020.pdf\n1' J. Tooker & K. Pearsons. (Aug 2021) Newer characters, same story: neonicotinoid insecticides disruptfood webs\nthrough direct and indirect efiects. Current Opinion on Insect Science Journal. doi.org/ 10.1016/j.cois.202 l .02.0l3\n\"Oliveira, et al. (Feb 2025) The impact of neonicotinoid insecticides on amphibians: A comprehensive review and\nfuture research challenges. Environmental Research Journal. doi.org/l0.1016Lj.env1'es.2024.120595 '3 C.M. Thompson. (April 2022) Carryover effects of pesticide exposure and pond drying on performance, behavior,\n\nand sex ratios in a pool breeding amphibian. Journal of Zoology. doi.org/10.11 l 1/jz0.l2975\n\n;i:mGii5iaritlFai':11 Titsziti, Faitr1.ou iii, iirisiiiiee :1.i.;g.�@ii1f; S\n\n(_:;2ii'vj} '_f'E;'1\"llZf_�j3{lT%\n\n1's1ai.11eatiduimxixsrg\n\nEndangered Species Risk\nAccording to a recent EPA review, three commonly used neonics--including irnidacloprid--pose a threat to 75% of all endangered plants and animals. Imidacloprid alone has been found to adversely\nimpact 1,445 species, including over 80% of federally endangered species.\" The study showed species such as the Northern Long-Eared Bat and Piping Plover, both listed in Maine, are at particularly high\nrisk.\n\nImpacts on Human and Mammal Health\nNeonics have long been considered to have low toxicity to mammals and humans, especially in\ncomparison to older insecticides such as organophosphates and carbamates. However, evidence\nincreasingly demonstrates that neonics do pose risks to mammalian and human health due to their persistence in the environment. Humans are continuously exposed to neonics through\ndrinking water, the food that we eat, or indoor air contamination following pest treatments.\n\nNeonics can persist in the environment for up to 19 years, making long term exposure a high risk, and epidemiological studies have confirmed that quantities of neonics have gradually increased over the last\ndecade in human samples. Exposure to these pesticides have been associated with headache, fatigue, convulsions, abdominal and chest pain, weakness, tremors, respiratory failure, and memory loss.\"\nA study in Minnesota found that imidacloprid was found at higher concentrations in the\nspleens ofwhite-tailed deer, leading to lethargy, smaller organ sizes, and potentially lower reproductive success.\" These effects pose risks to Maine's deer population and, by extension, its ecological and economic health.\n\nBirds\n\nEven a single treated corn kernel can kill a bird the size of a Blue\n\na .17\" Y\n\nBirds\n\nare\n\nPrimarilY\n\nexP osed to neonicotinoids through eatin5 treated insects, seeds, or Plants. ExP osure to even low levels\n\n14 US EPA. (Jun 2022). EPA Finalizes Biological Evaluations Assessing Potential Effects of Three Neonicotinoid\nPesticides on Endangered Species. EPA.\nyywwepa.govlpesticides/epa-finalizes-biological-evaluations-assessing-pote11tial-effects-three-neonicotinoid\n15 Ferreira & Faro. (Aug 2021). Neurotoxic Effects of Neonicotinoids on Mammals: What Is There beyond the\nActivation of Nicotinic Acetylcholine Receptors?--A Systematic Review. International Joumal of Molecular Sciences. www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/22/16/84 13\n16\nR. Beer. (Nov 2023). Study shows widespread neonicotinoid exposure in Minnesota white~tailed deer. Twin Cities\nPioneer Press.\nyvww_,t3/incitiescom/202 I/03/01gjudy-shows-widespread-neonicotinoid-exposure-in-minnesota-white-tailed-deerl\n17\nAnita, et al. (Jan 2015). Imidacloprid-treated seed ingestion has lethal effect on adult partridges and reduces both breeding investment and oflfspring immunity. Environmental Research Joumal. doi.org/10. 1016/j.envres.2014. 10.023\nla Mineau & Whiteside. (June 2021). Pesticide Acute Toxicity Is a Better Correlate of U.S.\nGrassland Bird Declines than Agricultural Intensification. PLOS One.\nboerenIandvogels.nl/sites/default/files/j 0u1'nal.pone_.005745 7%20Mineau%20and%20Whiteside.pdf\n\nGi3slantlFarn1Roz1d 7 Falniouth .~ Maine\n\nOi\n\n10�\"\n\\)\n\n;.30\"*' 78l~Zl'9'3'4:1\n\n/., .\n\n\\J-J\n\nI11&l118.UCl'll/lJCLl`l.OE?`\n<3\n\nof neonicotinoids can impair avian directional ability, impacting migration, and breeding. Neonics negatively impact bird reproduction, contributing to reduced chick survival, eggshell thinning, and low hatch and fertility rates.\" Widespread contamination has been linked to population declines in grassland and insectivorous species like songbirds, sparrows, and flycatchers. Furthermore, non-target insect declines from neonicotinoids can negatively impact bird populations that rely on them for sustenance, even if the birds themselves are not exposed.\"\n\nCornell's Wildlife Health Lab warns that birds ingesting neonic-coated seeds face acute toxicity, while chronic exposure reduces reproductive success and impairs navigation and foraging. The American Bird Conservancy has documented widespread contamination in bird habitats, threatening both migratory and resident species.\n\nPollinators:\nPollinators--vital to both Maine's ecosystems and agricultural economy---are in crisis, and neonics are\na primary driver. Neonics are 7,000 times more toxic to bees than DDT, banned in 1972.21]ust one\nteaspoon ofimidacloprid can kill 1.25 billion honeybees.\" 5.1 ppb ofclothianidin, 6.4 ppb of imidacloprid, or 5.1 ppb of thiamethoxam is sufficient to compromise honey bee reproductive function,\" reduce egg-laying by 50%,\" and cause queen death.\" Pollinators contribute tens of billions of dollars annually to U.S. agriculture, including key Maine crops like blueberries, apples, squash, and tomatoes.\" Their decline not only threatens biodiversity but also food security and rural economies.\n\nINVASIVE SPECIES MANAGEMENT\n\nDespite their incredible harms, there may be situations in which it is necessary to apply neonicotinoids in a controlled environment or ecological emergency. Invasive species that have become prevalent in\n\n1� Cornell University College of Veterinary Medicine. (n.d.) Neonicotinoid Toxicosis. Cornell University.\ncwbl.vet.cornell.edu/system:'files/public/cwhl-fact-sheets-neonic.pdf\n2� American Bird Conservancy. (July 2023). Use ofDeadly Pesticides Remains Unchecked in U.S., Despite\nDevastating Impact on Birds, Other Wildlife. ABC. https://abcbirds.org/news/2023-neonic-repo11/ 2` Raichel. (Sept 2022) Curb Neonic Use to Save Bees -- And Farmers. NRDC.\nhtlgps;Ziyyww,nrdc.oi;g[bjo/d_aniel-raichel/curb-neonic-use-sz11e;_bee�;gnd-farniers\n22 Goulson. (2014). Are crops being devastated without neonicotinoid protection ?. University of Sussex.\nw\\5Lw_.sussex.ac.uk/lifesci/goulsonlab/blog/neonicotinoicl-protection\n23 T.Grout, et al. (2020) Neonicotinoid Insecticides in New York State. Cornell University.\ncornell.agp.box.com/v/2020--neonicotinoid-report\n24 Smart & Spivak. (Aug 2016). Sub-lethal eflects ofdietary neonicotinoid insecticide exposure on honey bee queen\nfecundity and colony development. Nature Scientific Reports. www.nature.com/articles/srep32108 25 Williams, et al. (Oct 2015). Neonicotinoid pesticides severely aflect honey bee queens. Nature Scientific Reports.\nwww.nature.com/articles/step1462 l\n26 USDA Office of the Chief Scientist. (2022). USDA Annual Strategic Pollinator Priorities Report. USDA.\nhttps://Www.usda.gov/sites/default/iiles/documents/annual-pollinator-report-2022.pdf\n\n20 Giislzirzd FiiL\"H1Rl.'}1(i,�l` Eiil'I'l{>�_ll,li, liyliiflililtit (>4\n\n~\n\n(:20?) 731 .1.-:�;j;;1?;{:\n\nmaizaeaijzdtlbozii.<;�r�t;1\n\nMaine, such as Hemlock Woolly Adelgid or the Emerald Ash Borer, can be effectively managed using neonicotinoid treatment. The risks of applying neonics to affected species can and must be weighed with the cost of not addressing the invasive species or using alternative treatments, and should be\napplied using practices to minimize environmental and ecological harm. LD 1323 effectively outlines\nmethodologies to reach this goal through an extensive exemption process.\nLD 1323 is a science-backed, economically reasonable, and ecologically necessary policy that\nreflects growing awareness of the dangers posed by neonicotinoids. Maine has an opportunity to be a leader in safeguarding biodiversity, pollinator health, and sustainable agriculture.\nWe respectfully urge this committee to vote \"Ought to Pass\" on LD 1323. Thank you for your time\nand your commitment to protecting Maine's natural resources.\nSincerely,\nAnia Wright Policy Advocate\n\n13.0 Gilsland P316111 Road, Falniotith, i\\iie\\i11e 041055; W\n\n(:;<1>\"7) f781~~:2,{?,g0\n\nmainezu1<:lub0;ii.0i'g\n\nSincerely,\n\nAppalachian Mountain Club Center for an Ecology Based Economy\nConservation Law Foundation\nFriends of Casco Bay Mahoosuc Land Trust\nMaine Audubon\nMaine Association of Conservation Commissions\nMaine Conservation Voters Maine Council of Churches Maine Organic Farmers and Gardeners\nAssociation\nQ\n\"mmo ACT\n\nMaine Environmental Education Association\n\nMaine Public Health Association\n\nMaine Unitarian Universalist Support Action\nNetwork (MUUSAN)\nMaine Youth for Climate Justice\n\nNatural Resources Council of Maine\n\nPhysicians for Social Responsibility Maine Chapter\n\nSlingshot\n\nSierra Club Maine\n\nThe Climate Initiative\n\nThe Nature Conservancy\n\nThird Act Maine\n�\n\nMHJ A Maine Public Health Association\n\n0*\" Ar\": P4\n\nMKMSAN Ax w A\"\n\n2\n\n�-.\n\nTh@Nature A ~11\n\nConservanCY J \"\"@~i.a�`=�\n\n.--\\\n\n~\n\nMaine Unitarian Universalist Slate Mvunany italwmk\n\nC�\"5EF\\/\"\"0\"\n,\nLaw Foundation\n\nNatural Resources\n\nClimate\nInitiative\n\nC\nMaine\nCOI1SC1'V3.tiOl'1\n\nCouncil of Maine\n\nMaine Association of\n\n*\n\nonse\n\nQ\n\nP Voters -\n\n\"\nPh\n\nf sUCIE'I|nESPOIISI'b'li'lt)'\n\n__;il\nA Y\"i_Friends of Casco Bay\nCasco BAYKEEPER\n\n` Center for can 4, E��l�9Y'B�'5ed\n\n� MFIINE VOUTH FOR CLIMHTE JUSTICE\n\nmaine\n\nCommittee on Agriculture, Conservation, and Forestry\n% Legislative Information Office\n100 State House Station\nAugusta, ME 04333\n\nApril 1S, 2025\n\nRE: LD 1323, An Act to Prohibit the Use of Neonicotinoid Pesticides and the Use and Sale of\nNeonicotinoid-treated Seeds\nOn behalf of the environmental, conservation, and public health organizations listed below, please pass LD 1323, An Act to Pr0laz'bz't the Use ofNe0nz'c0tz'n0z'd Pestz'cz'de.r and the U36 and Sale qf\nNe0nz'c0tz'n0z'd--treated Seeds, to protect Maine farmers, pollinators, and our environment. As harmful neonicotinoids (`neonics') continue to be used in Maine, it is more important than ever to protect Maine farmers, pollinators, and our food system by prohibiting the use ofneonic treated\nl\nseeds and expanding the current list of prohibited uses of neonics.\nNeonics are a class of synthetic pesticides that are used widely due to their effectiveness as a neurotoxin\nto eliminate pests. They are commonly used on farmlands, lawns, gardens, and in flea and tick pet treatments. Unlike other pesticides, neonics are systemic, meaning that they are taken up by plants from the soil and become present in plant material and pollen. Neonics can also enter groundwater and can remain in the soil for many years. Neonic effectiveness is indiscriminate and they kill not only pest insects, but beneficial pollinators, other insects, and wildlife that eat ailected plants or insects. Neonicanatoid use is attributed to mass pollinator declines which threaten our food system and\nfarmers.\nGiven evidence ofworsening effects of widespread neonic use, encouraging evidence of farmers transitioning to non-neonic pesticides, and a growing movement toward non-neonic use\nregionally and worldwide, now is the time to expand Maine's current restrictions on neonicotinoid use. LD 1323 updates the current law to prohibit the application ofall neonics to\nornamental plants, plants during bloom, and certain cereal crops, as well as prohibiting the use of neonic coated soybean and cereal crop seeds.\nHerein Maine, we take pride in our pristine environment and abundant wildlife. We support family\nfarms, and trust that decision makers will protect their livelihood, along with the land we love and the\npollinators that are vital to our survival and the wildlife that depends on them. Please support LD\nl 323.\n\nCommittee on Agriculture, Conservation, and Forestry\n% Legislative Information Office\n100 State House Station\nAugusta, ME 04333\n\nApril 15, 2025\n\nRE: LD 1323, An Act to Prohibit the Use of Neonicotinoid Pesticides and the Use and Sale of Neonicotinoid-treated Seeds\nAs someone who cares deeply about Maine's environment, native wildlife, and the health of our\nfarms and food system, I urge you to support LD 1323, which would expand restrictions on the use of harmful neonicotinoid pesticides (\"neonics\").\nNeonics are a class of systemic pesticides that are toxic to bees, butterflies, birds, and other beneficial insects, contaminate water, and persist in soil for months or years. These chemicals are absorbed by plants and remain present in pollen and nectar, making them especially dangerous to pollinators critical to agriculture and natural ecosystems.\nDespite the passage of LD 155 in 2021, which limited certain non-essential uses of neonics,\nthese pesticides continue to threaten Maine's environment. Some companies coat seeds with neonics before planting, but over 90% of these chemicals end up in surrounding soil and water,\nproviding little to no benefit to farmers while devastating pollinator populations and disrupting\nthe ecological balance.\nLD 1323 is a necessary next step. lt would:\n0 Prohibit the use of neonics on ornamental plants, blooming crops, and certain food\ncrops;\nI Ban the use of neonic-coated soybean and cereal crop seeds, and; 0 Maintain emergency exemptions for agricultural and environmental crises.\nThis legislation aligns Maine with similar laws in New York, Vermont, Quebec, and the European\nUnion, and supports farmers by promoting safer, more sustainable growing practices.\nBy signing this petition, l stand with the people of Maine in calling for bold action to protect our native bees, our food system, and our shared environment. Please support LD 1323.\nSincerely,\n\nKatherine Reddoch Paul Benham\n\nKatherine Drenski\n\nJeffrey Rainey\n\nBradley Arnall\n\nLillie Vitelli\nMarisol Maddox Jesse McMahon\nDeborah Bloomer\n\nRuth Lantigua\nAna Macias\n\nSusan\nDana\n\nBeaulier\nLittle\n\nLinda DeSantis\n\nMarlis Whittier\n\nLinda Stearns\n\nMichele Benoit\nWende Mahaney James Morgan\n\nCarol Scott\n\nStephanie Lindsell\n\nRuth Poland\n\nDeborah Blank\nBabette Gwynn\nEdwin Hawkes Deborah Hawkes\n\nJudith Allen\n\nAdair\nSiri\n\nDeLamater Beckman\n\nJanet Armington\n\nKristna Evans\n\nRobert Grant\n\nLawrence O'Toole\n\nAlison Barkley\nMary Kaldenbaugh Christopher Groden\n\nSara Bennett\n\nNancy Tyndall Seth Benz\n\nKathy Margroff Marie Geary\n\nAlbany Twp ME Albion ME Alfred ME Andover ME Appleton ME Appleton ME Appleton ME Arrowsic ME Arundel ME Arundel ME Arundel ME Ashland ME Auburn ME Auburn ME Auburn ME Bangor ME Bangor ME Bangor ME Bangor ME Bango